  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Given that $D=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4,$ what is $D(y)$ for $y={x}^{3}?$",
  "worked_solution": "The given operator is a second-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y)={a}_{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+{a}_{1}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+{a}_{0}y\n$$\n\nwith constant coefficients  ${a}_{2}=1,{a}_{1}=-3,$ and ${a}_{0}=4.$ \n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}+4){x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}({x}^{3})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})+4\\cdot {x}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-3\\cdot (3{x}^{2})+4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =6x-9{x}^{2}+4{x}^{3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+6x\\end{aligned}\n$$",
